    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "i hear you. but it feels like \"pedagogical approach\" is kind of a fancy way of saying the school is just doing what they want regardless of what parents think. if the research shows it helps kids, doesn't that make it the objectively better choice for the school to implement it? like, shouldn't schools follow the science maybe? or is it just a matter of opinion?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "At the core of the debate is the tension between evidence-based best practice and the right of parents to direct the moral and social education of their children.\n\nAdvocates for following the \"science\" argue that social and emotional well-being should be a core, researched part of a comprehensive education, making it the objective best choice for supporting all students.\n\nConversely, many argue that while the *outcome* (a supportive child) is good, the *method* of social instruction is fundamentally a matter of deeply held personal and religious opinion, which they believe the state should not mandate in this way.\n\nTherefore, for the school, it is often a complex political balance between adhering to evolving educational best practices and respecting diverse community values and legal mandates.",
